Catalog Doctor - third-party inspiration notice
==============================================

Catalog Doctor is an original implementation. No source code was copied from the
projects listed below. They are credited because their public documentation and
domain modelling informed the design decisions noted next to each name.

* Bagisto (https://github.com/bagisto/bagisto, MIT)
  Idea borrowed: category-scoped attribute sets, where required fields are
  declared per category instead of globally.

* django-oscar (https://github.com/django-oscar/django-oscar, BSD-3-Clause)
  Idea borrowed: separating catalogue structure (category, product class,
  product attributes) from stock and pricing concerns.

* Saleor (https://github.com/saleor/saleor, BSD-3-Clause)
  Idea borrowed: attribute input types (dropdown / numeric / boolean) drive
  validation, and validation errors carry a machine-readable code.

* dotnet/eShop (https://github.com/dotnet/eShop, MIT)
  Idea borrowed: layered reference architecture, keeping domain rules free of
  transport and persistence details.

* marqo-ai/marqo (https://github.com/marqo-ai/marqo, Apache-2.0)
  Idea borrowed: the observation that near-duplicate merchandise detection is a
  matching problem over noisy text, not an exact-key problem.

* Aimeos (https://github.com/aimeos/aimeos-laravel, MIT)
  Idea borrowed: a per-attribute "list type" concept (required / optional)
  surfaced directly in the admin experience.

All brand names, dataset values and category taxonomies used by this project are
original and synthetic.

